{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg3Q3BtRrch598pG1t9iv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[x] Hydration suite: for each of the three markers, the marker is found in its named server build file (positive control), and the test reports the control file by name.\n[x] Hydration suite: for each marker, every JavaScript asset under the client build is read and asserted to lack it, with one assertion per file.\n[x] Hydration suite: a marker taken from a kept component's markup (Badge or Notice) is found in at least one client asset.\n[x] Hydration suite: a marker that appears in no server file fails the positive control with a message saying the marker is stale (verified by a temporary bogus marker during development, not committed).\n\nThe hydration suite now checks that stripped markup and server-only code stay out of the built client JS, and all four checklist items are done. The ticket's blocker no longer applies: `npm run build:hydration` succeeds at HEAD.\n\nThe new tests are in `test/hydration.ts`. Each of the three markers is first checked against a server file; if it's missing there, the test fails with \"marker is stale: not found in <file>\" and skips the client checks. Otherwise every JS file under the client build gets its own pass/fail line. The markers are:\n\n1. **Counter:** `aria-label=\"Decrement\"`, which appears only in `build/server/app.js`.\n2. **Scaffold:** the page marker `<!--microtags:main-->`, checked against `build/server/app.js`. It is also in the client `index.html`, but that isn't a JS asset, so the scan doesn't read it.\n3. **Build entry:** `microtags/build requires \"typescript\"`. Nothing in the example's server build contains the TypeScript transform, so I used `dist/build.js` as this marker's server file instead. `build:hydration` builds it first. This is my reading of a ticket that assumed the string would be in the server build, and a comment on the table says so.